let __v_262 = Value::from("It is printed on a sticker on the back or underneath the router, unless someone changed it. A phone that has joined the wifi can show it too: on an iPhone tap the i beside the network in Settings > Wifi, and on Android it is in the network's Share menu.");
frame.store("WIFI_STICKER", __v_262.clone())?;
let __v_263 = Value::from("Log in to the router - the address, a username and a password are on the sticker on the back, usually 192.168.0.1 or 192.168.1.1 - then open Wireless or Wifi settings, type the new password and save. Every device drops off and needs the new one, so do it when that is not a problem, and write it on the sticker.");
frame.store("WIFI_CHANGE", __v_263.clone())?;
let __v_264 = Value::from("If the password on the sticker does not get you into the router, someone changed it: the way back is the reset hole - with the router on, push a paperclip into the hole marked Reset for 10 to 30 seconds until the lights blink, and it goes back to the sticker's password. That also wipes the wifi name and password you set, so every device needs joining again. Change the admin password on the way back in and write it on the sticker.");
frame.store("WIFI_RESET", __v_264.clone())?;
let __v_265 = Value::from("Use the reset hole, not the plug: with the router on, push a paperclip or a pin into the small hole marked Reset (or hold the Reset button) for 10 to 30 seconds, until the lights blink or the router restarts, and it goes back to the factory settings on the sticker - including the wifi name and password, so every device needs joining again. Unplugging it for 30 seconds is only a reboot and keeps your settings, so try that first if it is just dropping out. Some provider routers reset from their app instead (Sky, BT and Virgin all have a button in the app), and a router they gave you may come back with the settings it shipped with.");
frame.store("ROUTER_FACTORY", __v_265.clone())?;
let __v_266 = Value::from("Up high, out in the open and as close to the middle of the flat as the cable allows - on a shelf or the top of a bookcase, not on the floor, in a cupboard, behind the TV or beside the microwave, a cordless phone or the hot water tank. Radio waves spread out sideways and downwards from the box, so one in a corner mostly covers the corner. The line comes in where it comes in: if that is one end, keep the router there and add a mesh unit at the other end rather than running a long cable.");
frame.store("_ROUTER_WHERE", __v_266.clone())?;
